WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.740
If you could teleport the Earth

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.608
to any place in the Universe,

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.110
what would you want to look at

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.778
in the night sky?

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:14.414
The bright explosion of a supernova?

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:17.283
A black hole passing over your house?

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.152
How about a nebula?

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.528
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.896
and here's what would happen

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.965
if we lived inside of a nebula.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.102
What is a nebula anyway?

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:39.439
A nebula is a giant cloud of dust and gas

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.374
in interstellar space.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.842
They are vast,

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:46.346
sometimes taking up hundreds
of light-years of space.

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:49.983
But with all that size,
they aren't massive.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:52.185
A nebula the diameter of the Earth

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:55.588
would only have a mass of a few kilograms.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:59.025
That's because nebulae aren't very dense.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:01.594
And that's why, if we lived inside one,

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:04.497
it wouldn't look anything
like what you might expect.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:08.868
Some 1,344 light years away from Earth,

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.503
is the Orion Nebula -

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.906
the brightest nebula in our sky.

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:15.041
It's 24 light-years across,

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:18.745
and has a mass equivalent to 2,000 Suns.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.980
If the Earth was formed inside it,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.818
all you'd see in the night sky would be this.

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:25.985
Not impressed?

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.554
I know, I wasn't either.

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:29.756
It turns out it's not that
easy to see a nebula

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.891
when you look at the night sky.

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:34.594
To your eye, most galaxies in the Universe

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.830
don't look anything like this.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.698
More like this.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:42.035
And most nebulae are invisible altogether.

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:46.272
Your eyes aren't sensitive to the
kind of light a nebula produces.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.476
But look at the image taken by
the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:52.378
and you'll see a beautiful gas cloud. 

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:55.849
That doesn't mean these beautiful
images of space are fake.

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.284
Their colors just represent the lightwaves

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.687
that your eyes aren't able to detect.

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:03.990
So, if you had the Hubble Telescope,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.193
would you be able to see a
nebula if you lived in one?

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:10.663
Sorry to disappoint you again, but no.

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:14.634
Nebulae are pretty only when they
are hundreds of light-years away.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.703
They look thick and cloudy only because 

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:19.973
you see the structures
of light-years in thickness.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:22.976
Once you're close to them, their
light spreads out so much

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.577
that you can't see them.

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.280
You could fly your spaceship
right into the nebula

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.082
and never even know it.

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:31.151
If the Earth were inside a nebula,

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:34.521
the only effect you'd see
would be brighter Auroras.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:36.856
Unless the solar wind blew
all the nebular particles

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.391
far away from the planet.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:43.129
How do we know we are not living
inside a nebula right now?

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:44.430
Simple.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.066
After gathering the data on our own Sun,

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:51.437
we can calculate the expected
brightness of the neighboring stars.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.606
If we did live inside a nebula,

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:57.744
we'd notice that our star was
much brighter compared to others.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:03:02.415
The nebula would be dimming the
light coming in from outside of it.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:03.783
But don't be sad.

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.386
We can't teleport the Earth, yet.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:09.522
But we may be able to travel
to distant exoplanets

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:13.159
that experience incredible
light shows in the night sky.

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:17.197
But that's a story for another WHAT IF.

